{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 346 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "a1a618ba652a5cfb53ef084a7d0d7f574d1a965ce324c9a9ce4189fd9d370d69" :edb/db-before-t 345 :edb/db-after-t 346 :edb/tx-data [[13194139533658 50 #inst "2026-10-04T07:25:51.273Z" 13194139533658 true] [17592186110680 1020 #edb/ref 17592186244718 13194139533658 true] [17592186110680 1020 #edb/ref 17592186244725 13194139533658 true] [17592186110680 1020 #edb/ref 17592186244732 13194139533658 true] [17592186110680 1020 #edb/ref 17592186244767 13194139533658 true] [17592186110680 1020 #edb/ref 17592186244795 13194139533658 true] [17592186110681 1020 #edb/ref 17592186244739 13194139533658 true] [17592186110681 1020 #edb/ref 17592186244774 13194139533658 true] [17592186110681 1020 #edb/ref 17592186244781 13194139533658 true] [17592186110681 1020 #edb/ref 17592186244788 13194139533658 true] [17592186110681 1020 #edb/ref 17592186244802 13194139533658 true] [17592186110682 1020 #edb/ref 17592186244746 13194139533658 true] [17592186110682 1020 #edb/ref 17592186244809 13194139533658 true] [17592186110682 1020 #edb/ref 17592186244816 13194139533658 true] [17592186110682 1020 #edb/ref 17592186244837 13194139533658 true] [17592186110684 1020 #edb/ref 17592186244753 13194139533658 true] [17592186110684 1020 #edb/ref 17592186244760 13194139533658 true] [17592186110684 1020 #edb/ref 17592186244823 13194139533658 true] [17592186110684 1020 #edb/ref 17592186244830 13194139533658 true] [17592186110825 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533658 true] [17592186110825 1145 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931905/assets/734eef45985ddfb6450666e1dd32c6d3beab9ca3aef66c87c6ba393d6960b08b.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos⁡\\theta ,\\,y=sin⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{1}{5}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\nsin⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $cos⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{5}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{1}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{24}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $cos⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So, $cos⁡\\theta =\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{5}.$\n\nKnowing that $sec⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{cos⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\nsec⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{5})}=\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt{6}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we rationalize the denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sec⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt{6}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt{6}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\sqrt{6}}{2\\cdot 6} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\sqrt{6}}{12}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533658 true] [17592186111269 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533658 true] [17592186111269 1145 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931905/assets/21170c11aa5ff0ab39b86f94588e5e8a0fa178d0c34b9aa1774ee9f07a4d0812.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos⁡\\theta ,\\,y=sin⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{7}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\nsin⁡\\theta =\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{7}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $cos⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{7}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{7})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{45}{49}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{4}{49}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{2}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $cos⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So, $cos⁡\\theta =\\frac{2}{7}.$\n\nFinally, knowing that $tan⁡\\theta =\\frac{sin⁡\\theta }{cos⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\ntan⁡\\theta =\\frac{(\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{7})}{(\\frac{2}{7})}=\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{2}.\n$$" 13194139533658 true] [17592186244718 1017 #edb/ref 17592186244719 13194139533658 true] [17592186244718 1089 "q-124608" 13194139533658 true] [17592186244718 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533658 true] [17592186244718 1100 "The point $A$ is located in the first quadrant and lies on the unit circle. The $y$-coordinate of $A$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}.$ What is the $x$-coordinate of $A?$" 13194139533658 true] [17592186244718 1113 #uuid "c31e5dd5-162f-5697-8eab-a49856c3ff0a" 13194139533658 true] [17592186244718 1138 false 13194139533658 true] [17592186244718 1145 "Since the point $A$ lies on the unit circle, it must satisfy the Pythagorean theorem: \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}$ into the above and solving for $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3})}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+(\\frac{2}{9}) & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =1-\\frac{2}{9} \\\\ {x}^{2} & =\\frac{7}{9} \\\\ x & =\\sqrt{\\frac{7}{9}} \\\\ x & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A$ lies in the first quadrant, $x$ must be positive, so we select the positive answer. Therefore, our final answer is $x=\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{3}.$" 13194139533658 true] [17592186244719 1000 #edb/ref 17592186244720 13194139533658 true] [17592186244719 1000 #edb/ref 17592186244721 13194139533658 true] [17592186244719 1000 #edb/ref 17592186244722 13194139533658 true] [17592186244719 1000 #edb/ref 17592186244723 13194139533658 true] [17592186244719 1000 #edb/ref 17592186244724 13194139533658 true] [17592186244719 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533658 true] [17592186244719 1041 "selecti